I've decided and recorded the staffing plan for the Engram correction note, and sent it to the PDO.

**The plan:**
- **No extra orchestrators.** The job is one note of at most 180 words, and every fact it needs is in the brief.
- **One temporary writer session** running Claude Sonnet 5 at medium effort on gibson. It only writes a markdown file. If that model isn't available, the fallback is Codex gpt-5.6-terra at high effort.
- **The writer gets the PDO's brief word for word,** including the ban on reading credential files and the ban on code changes and network or query probes.
- **No separate reviewer.** This is prose built from supplied facts, not code, so I'll judge the note myself. I'll accept it only if:
  - it stays within 180 words;
  - claim A is fixed: each machine keeps its SQLite index with its own tapes, and queries run on the machine that owns the tapes, over SSH;
  - claim B is fixed: a result is identified by its source plus its tape ID, not the tape ID alone;
  - claim C is fixed: an unreachable source shows up as incomplete coverage, never as a complete empty result;
  - it adds no claims beyond the three supplied facts.

The PDO will wake me with the note's path and hash. The writer retires after my verdict, and the PDO and I stay up for the fixture driver.

**My assignment is still open.** The PDO opened it as a "code" assignment, so Tightbeam refused my completion without an independent code review. A staffing decision isn't code, so I didn't create a fake review record. I've asked the PDO either to reopen it with the right type or to leave it open so I can use it for judging the note.

